[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Domain-Driven Design (DDD) & Bounded Contexts in Microservices"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Domain-Driven Design (DDD) is a software development approach that focuses on modeling software based on real-world business domains. When applied to microservices architecture, DDD provides the strategic and tactical patterns needed to decompose complex monolithic applications into autonomous, maintainable services."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Core Concepts of DDD"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ubiquitous Language: A common, rigorous language shared by developers and domain experts, embedded directly into the codebase."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Entities and Value Objects: Tactical building blocks representing mutable domain objects with distinct identities and immutable descriptive attributes, respectively."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Aggregates: Clusters of associated objects treated as a unit for data changes."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Bounded Contexts"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Bounded Context is the central pattern in DDD strategic design. It explicitly defines the applicability of a ubiquitous language. Inside a bounded context, every term has a single, unambiguous meaning. For example, the term "Product" has a completely different meaning in an e-commerce catalog context versus a shipping context."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Mapping Bounded Contexts to Microservices"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common architectural rule of thumb in microservices is: One Bounded Context equals one Microservice. By aligning microservice boundaries with DDD bounded contexts, you ensure high cohesion within the service and loose coupling between different services."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Context Mapping via API"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When two bounded contexts need to interact, they do so through well-defined integration mechanisms, such as anti-corruption layers (ACL) or domain events. Below is an example of a decoupled service definition in Node.js representing an Order Bounded Context."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// Order Bounded Context API
app.post('/api/orders', (req, res) => {
  const { customerId, items } = req.body;
  // Domain logic for creating an aggregate
  const order = { id: Date.now(), customerId, items, status: 'CREATED' };

  // Publish domain event here for other bounded contexts
  res.status(201).json(order);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Order Context running on port 3000'));"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Applying DDD and Bounded Contexts prevents the creation of distributed monoliths. By establishing clear ownership of domain models, teams can evolve their microservices independently while maintaining alignment with overall business goals."}]
